  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Nomada articulata, commonly known as the Articulated Nomad bee, is a small cuckoo bee in the family Apidae found throughout New England. These parasitic bees lay their eggs in the nests of other bee species, particularly sweat bees, rather than constructing their own nests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Body segmentation",
      "description": "Distinctive articulated (jointed) appearance with clearly separated body segments, giving it a segmented or 'beaded' look",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Tiny bee, typically 5-7mm in length, much smaller than honey bees and most bumble bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Coloration pattern",
      "description": "Reddish-brown to black body with distinctive yellow or pale markings on the abdomen in a banded pattern",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Nomada articulata inhabits open woodlands, gardens, and meadows where its host bee species are abundant, thriving in areas with diverse wildflowers and nesting sites.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a cuckoo bee, Nomada articulata is a 'brood parasite' that sneaks into host bee nests to lay its eggs, where the larvae are raised by the unsuspecting host bee mother.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
